# 5. Notification Event

Here's an important distinction.

We have a **database entity**:

```text
Notification
```

But Kafka carries an **event**:

```text
NotificationEvent
```

They aren't necessarily the same thing.

Our Kafka event will contain the information the consumer needs:

```json
{
  "notificationId": "notification-123",
  "userId": "user-101",
  "channel": "IN_APP",
  "title": "Flight Delayed",
  "message": "Your flight BA123 has been delayed.",
  "createdAt": "2026-08-15T21:10:00"
}
```

Think of it like:

```text
Database Entity
      ↓
"What is this notification?"

Kafka Event
      ↓
"What happened that other services need to know?"
```

This distinction is important for event-driven architecture.

---

# 6. Kafka topic

We'll initially have one main topic:

```text
notifications
```

Flow:

```text
Notification API
      │
      ▼
notifications topic
      │
      ▼
Notification Consumer
```

Later we'll introduce:

```text
notifications
notifications.DLT
```

for failed messages.

---

# 7. Channels

We'll support two real channels.

### IN_APP

```text
Kafka
  ↓
Consumer
  ↓
WebSocket
  ↓
Browser
```

### EMAIL

```text
Kafka
  ↓
Consumer
  ↓
Email Channel
  ↓
Local SMTP
  ↓
Email testing inbox
```

And our architecture will allow:

```text
NotificationChannel
       │
       ├── InAppNotificationChannel
       └── EmailNotificationChannel
```

This will give us a natural opportunity to learn the **Strategy Pattern** later.

We won't build Push right now.

---

# 8. Failure scenario

This is where the project becomes interesting.

Suppose email sending fails:

```text
Kafka
  ↓
Consumer
  ↓
Email Service
  X
```

We don't immediately mark it permanently failed.

Instead:

```text
Attempt 1
   ↓
FAIL
   ↓
Retry
   ↓
Attempt 2
   ↓
FAIL
   ↓
Retry
   ↓
Attempt 3
   ↓
FAIL
   ↓
DLQ
```

We'll implement this properly later.

